Scree-Tektite

Ingredients: PUNK ROCK + GABBER + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Didgeridoo
Creative Catalyst: Born at an illegal rooftop rave in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Scree-Tektite sounds like a localized earthquake occurring inside a flooded cathedral. It violently splices the spit-drenched, four-chord fury of 1970s hardcore punk with the punishing 200 BPM distorted kick drums of vintage gabber, all held together by the sub-bass drone of hollowed-out titanium didgeridoos. Floating over this hyper-violent rhythmic floor are eerie, pitch-shifted phantom vocals—recorded via hydrophones submerged in brackish monsoon runoff—that drift through the mix like lost signals from dead satellites. 

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 190–220 BPM. A relentlessly brutal 4-on-the-floor gabber kick overlaid with frantic, syncopated punk snare-cracks and circular-breathed didgeridoo sub-rhythms that create an unsettling, rolling momentum.
*   Harmonic Palette: Minor second intervals, phrygian dominant modes, and microtonal drone tones anchored to a deep, resonant low-frequency fundamental.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Hydro-Resonant Reverb:* Lead vocals and guitars are played into underwater acoustic chambers filled with floodwater to create an agonizing, swimming spatial tail.
       Kicker-Tear Overdrive:* The master bus is smashed through overdriven analog distortion units until the kick drum's transient produces a white-noise tear in the stereo image.
       Drone-Gate Triggers:* Low-end didgeridoo frequencies act as a sidechain trigger, ducking the screeching, rusted-metal punk guitar chords in a hypnotic, suffocating pump.
*   Dynamics: Suffocating, wall-to-wall noise density punctuated by sudden, dead-silent "drowning drops" where all high frequencies cut out, leaving only an ominous, reverberating didgeridoo humming in a void.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the monsoon season of 2091, as the black, oil-slicked waters of the tides swallowed the lower eighty floors of the Neo-Jakarta Spire Grid, thousands of disenfranchised youth fled upward. The culture erupted on the slippery tar-and-gravel roofs of the dry towers. Clad in heavy-duty vinyl diving drysuits decorated with neon safety-paint, rusted safety pins, and scavenged oxygen masks, these "Roof-Rats" threw sprawling, off-grid all-nighters under storm-wracked skies. To combat the deafening roar of industrial rain-pumps, performers wired high-output didgeridoos into modified Gabber sound-systems, screaming anarchist mantras through wet filter masks. The central ritual, known as "The Siphon," involves crowds slamming together in knee-deep rooftop puddles while circular-breathing performers blow low-frequency didgeridoo bursts directly into the standing water, causing the floor beneath their feet to physically hum and ripple.

Key Instrumentation

*   Titanium Cyber-Didgeridoo: 8-foot-long recycled alloy pipes fitted with piezo-electric throat pickups and digital pitch-shifters, producing subsonic sub-bass and haunting vocal overtones.
*   The Screaming Board: A modified fender stratocaster strung with rusted piano wire, dragged across metal railings and run through hyper-distorted fuzz boxes.
*   Roland TR-909 (Circuit-Bent): Pushed far beyond standard overdrive limits to produce the genre’s signature brain-rattling gabber kick-drum.
*   Hydro-Piezos: Underwater contact microphones capturing the gurgling, spooky ambience of the flooded city below.

